   133	No matched statement, stable statement identifier, contributor list,
   134	denial-reason collection, or third decision state is exposed.
   135	
   136	When policy does not permit issuance, the protocol boundary separately asks
   137	whether the policy supports the Repository Resource and Requested Permissions.
   138	Target support is a Boolean query over Repository Resource Constraints.
   139	Requested Permissions support uses permission coverage for that Repository
   140	Resource without considering OIDC Subject Token Constraints. Neither is an
   141	authorization result, and neither can permit issuance. An unsupported Repository Resource maps to RFC 8693
   142	`invalid_target`; unsupported Requested Permissions map to OAuth `invalid_scope`;
   143	when both are supported, the subject token is unacceptable to policy and maps
   144	to `invalid_request`.
   145	
   146	### Trust Subject Token Claims
   147	
   148	Successful OIDC validation authenticates the complete Subject Token Claims from one
   149	exact configured issuer for the cyspbot audience. Policy directly selects the
   150	Subject Token Claims that are material to authorization; it does not reconstruct
   151	one signed representation from another merely to check their consistency.
   152	
   153	GitHub Actions policy therefore selects exact signed `repository`,
   154	`event_name`, `ref_type`, `ref`, and `workflow_ref` values without constraining
   155	`sub`, `repository_id`, or `repository_owner_id` unless one becomes an actual
   156	policy key. GitHub supports replacing the complete default `sub` format with a
   157	configured Claim template, so treating the default format as a mandatory
   158	consistency checksum rejects legitimate tokens without adding protection
   159	against payload tampering or issuer compromise.
   160	
   161	The Fly provider capability uses no additional OIDC ID Token Profile. The
   162	former checks that `org_name` matched the issuer organization slug and that
   163	`sub` reconstructed from `org_name`, `app_name`, and `machine_name` are
   164	removed. If a reviewed application composition configures a Fly registration
   165	and Permit Statement, that statement directly selects whichever Subject Token
   166	Claims are material to its authorization. Missing or differently typed selected
   167	Claims make the statement not applicable rather than making an otherwise
   168	validly signed token fail authentication.
   169	
   170	GitHub Actions retains its Authorized Party check. Google retains `azp == sub`
   171	as a token-kind discriminator separating the accepted service-account token
   172	shape from Google user ID Tokens under the same Issuer Identifier.
   173	
   174	### Authentication and authorization remain separate
   175	
   176	OIDC Provider Registrations remain checked-in authentication decisions.
   177	Application composition verifies that every Issuer Identifier referenced by
   178	policy has a configured Provider Registration. The reverse is not required:
   179	registration alone creates no Permit Statement and grants no token-issuance
   180	permission.
   181	
   182	Policy refers to the exact Issuer Identifier carried by a Provider
   183	Registration. Provider-specific policy matchers, inheritance, issuer aliases,
   184	registration-derived capabilities, and automatic statement generation are not
   185	introduced. Ordinary TypeScript constants, arrays, spreads, `map`, and
   186	`flatMap` provide authoring reuse, including the capability to construct
   187	parameterized checked-in Fly registrations and statements.
   188	
   189	### Scope
   190	
   191	The generic OIDC Subject Token Constraint remains inside the specialized Token
   192	Issuance Policy module. It is not extracted to a shared package until a second
   193	real authorization consumer establishes a shared seam.
   194	
   195	The design does not create a general principal/action/resource framework,
   196	Boolean expression engine, role model, fragment system, policy inheritance
   197	mechanism, callback registry, or service-specific base implementation.
   198	
   199	## Consequences
   200	
   201	- CEL and its direct dependencies, bindings, caches, helpers, and
   202	  expression-specific tests are removed.
   203	- Policy authoring exposes only operations required by checked-in policy.
   204	- Weaker levels in the Requested Permissions are permitted by stronger
   205	  configured levels.
   206	- Permission names remain extensible; checked-in Permit Statements must still
   207	  cover every Requested Permission, and GitHub validates name and level
   208	  compatibility after policy approval.
   209	- Independently authored applicable statements contribute to the same Effective
   210	  Permissions without changing the request sent to GitHub.
   211	- The authorization model is deliberately closed under pointwise permission
   212	  union.
   213	- GitHub Actions policy, and any configured Fly policy, trust verified Subject
   214	  Token Claims rather than imposing redundant cross-Claim consistency
   215	  checks.
   216	- A policy result that does not permit issuance is direct Boolean control flow.
   217	- Operational logs retain the verified subject-token and normalized request
   218	  context but contain only the Boolean policy outcome.
   219	- Adding an OIDC Provider Registration does not authorize it; independent
   220	  Permit Statements remain necessary.
   221	- The opaque compiled representation permits future internal optimization
   222	  without changing authoring or evaluation interfaces.
   223	
   224	## Rejected alternatives

